A||||12||Ferrari_cdn||Horsepower and Torque||||11||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||09-24-2001||11:10 PM||||[dumb hat]<P>What is the difference between horsepower and torque?  I was just told a simple explanation before then at different times, that it meant the power of the engine.<P>You willing to explain me?<P>[/dumb hat]<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_2.JPG">  <BR>'84 2m4, 4 speed, 106k miles (157k km)  AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||11
Z||000001||Formula88||09-24-2001||11:38 PM||||Torque is a measure of how much work is being done.  How many pounds can be moved for so many feet.  That's where the term foot-pound came from.<P>Horsepower is a measure of how fast that work is done.  If it takes 1HP to do x amount of work in y amount of time, 2HP will do the same amount of work twice as fast.<P>In the automotive world, Horsepower is indirectly measured.  Torque output of the engine is directly measured, and then mathematically converted to Horsepower.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||walsh2001||09-25-2001||12:35 AM||||Isn't torque a leverage measurment of twisting force? Foot-pound means the force (in pounds) that would be felt at the end of 1 foot long lever attached to a turning rod. You could also therefore measure torque in... meter-pounds, centimeter-grams, ie... <P>------------------<BR>'87 Fiero GT ... Cherry Red||||reg||5
Z||000003||Standard||09-25-2001||12:52 AM||||Don't forget Newton-Meters  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Torque is what matters in the stoplight to stoplight drags  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  On the freeway is where horsepower come's into play.  My quad (180HP 165 ft/lb torque) isn't much faster than my GT (130 hp 170 ft/lb torque) in short, stoplight to stoplight runs.  But because the quad had ~50 more horse, above 30mph I start to walk away.  If you can find two cars with about the same amount of torque, but different levels of HP drive 'em both.  Pretty interesting, especially on the freeway.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">         <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||09-25-2001||01:08 AM||||Torque is what a motor DOES with the fuel. HP is basically [Tq * RPM * some constant], I can't remember the equation. Someone want to post it? The C is something like x\5.813 ?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000005||artherd||09-25-2001||02:26 AM||||Torque is measured as the static brake force (in pounts) applied by a stick 1-foot long attached to your crank shaft.<P>Horsepower is just torque times speed times 5252.<P>Both torque and horsepower numbers for cars are given as "peak" numbers.<P>(all units are lbs, and RPM)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||09-25-2001||08:44 AM||||HP sells cars, torque wins races... <P>------------------<BR>0-60 in 4.91||||reg||1
Z||000007||DrDave||09-25-2001||10:23 AM||||I'd much rather have an engine with 625 foot pounds of torque and 188 horses than 350 horses and 325 foot pounds of torque. (in fact I did make that choice and I loved it)||||reg||1
Z||000008||redfiero||09-25-2001||11:04 AM||||Horsepower is how fast you hit the wall.....Torque is how hard you hit it!||||reg||12
Z||000009||Formula88||09-25-2001||02:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by walsh2001:<BR><B>Isn't torque a leverage measurment of twisting force? Foot-pound means the force (in pounds) that would be felt at the end of 1 foot long lever attached to a turning rod. You could also therefore measure torque in... meter-pounds, centimeter-grams, ie... <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Correct - just from a different perspective.  The force of 1 pound of force at the end of a 1 foot long lever is one foot-pount.  It's just another way of looking at it. The equations are the same.  Horsepower would be how fast you're turning the lever, not how much force you're exerting on the lever.  Obviously, you need more force to make the lever move faster.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||09-25-2001||07:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>HP sells cars, torque wins races...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I'll have to remember that next time some dork in a Honda is talking sh!t.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||DRH||09-25-2001||09:33 PM||||Here's a link to an article discussing the HP and Torque:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.vettenet.org/torquehp.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.vettenet.org/torquehp.html</A> <P><BR>HP = Torque * RPM / 5252<P>Advertised HP and Torque numbers are the peak the engine puts out. It gives some indication of what to expect but doesn't tell the whole story. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||artherd||09-25-2001||11:26 PM||||Are you INSANE?!?<P>I'll take a 400bhp Ferrari with around 300lb-ft of torque (peak) ANY day.<P>How fast does your engine turn, 800rpm?<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DrDave:<BR><B>I'd much rather have an engine with 625 foot pounds of torque and 188 horses than 350 horses and 325 foot pounds of torque. (in fact I did make that choice and I loved it)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
